  "id": "Compound::DB00517 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Anisotropine Methylbromide appears to share structural and functional similarities with homatropine and cyclopentolate, all of which are antimuscarinic agents that bind to the cholinergic receptor subtype Chrm1. The evidence suggests that Anisotropine Methylbromide may exert its effects through a mechanism of action similar to those of its structural relatives, potentially by inhibiting the cholinergic receptor or modulating signaling pathways downstream of cholinergic activation. The presence of common ancestors in the network, such as Organonitrogen Compound and Antimuscarinic Agent, supports the idea that Anisotropine Methylbromide is part of a class of compounds with a shared biological profile. The final scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in the relationship, but the evidence is not strong enough to definitively establish a direct mechanism of action. The integration of structural similarities and functional relationships suggests that Anisotropine Methylbromide may act through a mechanism involving antimuscarinic activity, potentially affecting Chrm1 by modulating cholinergic signaling.",
